        "description": { "type": "literal" , "datatype": "http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/LatexString" , "value": "In the ISO System of Quantities, the $\\textit{Planck function}$ is defined by the formula $Y = -G/T$,\n    where $G$ is Gibbs energy and $T$ is thermodynamic temperature. It is a thermodynamic potential divided by temperature (a\n    \"free entropy\"), expressed in joules per kelvin, and is the Gibbs-energy counterpart of the Massieu function $J = -A/T$. The\n    same name is also widely used in radiometry for the spectral radiance $B(T)$ emitted by a black body, given by Planck's law;\n    that is a different quantity, of a different dimension, and is covered by quantitykind:SpectralRadiance.\n  " } ,
        "wikidata": { "type": "uri" , "value": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q76364998" } ,
        "definedBy": { "type": "uri" , "value": "http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind" }
